  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 934 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nCOOL AND MOSTLY DRY THROUGH THE WEEKEND:\n\nHigh pressure remains in control through much of the weekend, \nkeeping things dry and sunny. Temperatures remain on the cooler side \nthrough the weekend, with highs near to around 5 degrees below \nnormal and seasonably cool lows. Across the northwest CO valleys, as \nwell as the upper Colorado River Valley and CO Zone 23, subfreezing \ntemperatures are once again forecast. A Freeze Warning is in effect \nthrough 9AM Saturday morning. \n\nUNSETTLED WEATHER RETURNS:\n\nAn area of low pressure that has been lurking off the SoCal coast \nthe last few days is expected to become a mobile, open wave by \nSunday, making its way toward the Four Corners. A bit of moisture a \nlift associated with the passing of this wave will result in some \nlight mountain showers for the San Juans, generally in the form of \nrain. Some light snow showers will be possible Sunday night into \nMonday, but mainly at elevations above roughly 12k feet. Elsewhere, \nconditions remain dry.\n\nThe next stronger Pacific trough will approach the West Coast \nMonday, resulting in flow across the western CONUS turning \nsouthwesterly. This will begin to draw up rich tropical moisture \nthrough the Four Corners, leading to afternoon mountain showers \nwhere orographics are favorable, mainly across the San Juans and \ninto the Central Divide Mountains. Better forcing looks to move in \nmid-week, as the Pacific low moves through SoCal and begins to track \nthrough the Desert Southwest. There is still a lot of uncertainty \nregarding this system, its exact track, timing, and strength. \nSuffice to say, it's looking like a return to cooler and unsettled \nweather is likely mid to late next week.",
